# RentAI — Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
RentAI is a professional SaaS platform for short-term rental property managers.
It provides an AI-powered assistant (text + voice), a knowledge base per property,
chat history, guest communication, and a full management dashboard.

## Monorepo Structure & Tooling

```
/
├── backend/          # NestJS API (port 3000)
├── frontend-user/    # Next.js — manager/owner app (port 3012)
├── admin/            # Next.js — superadmin panel (port 3001)
├── landing/          # Next.js or Vite — marketing site (tech TBD)
└── packages/
    └── shared/       # Shared Zod schemas, TS types, utils
```

- **Package manager:** pnpm (strictly use `pnpm` for all commands; never `npm` or `yarn`)
- **Workspaces:** pnpm workspaces (`pnpm-workspace.yaml` at root)
- **Turborepo:** add when needed for build orchestration; not required initially
- **`packages/shared`:** imported via `workspace:*` as raw TypeScript; no build step during development. Add `tsup` only when explicit `dist/` or package publication is needed.
- Honor root `package.json` `"engines"` and `.nvmrc` if present. Do not introduce features requiring a newer Node than specified.

---

## Tech Stack

### Backend (NestJS)
- Node.js + TypeScript (strict mode)
- NestJS ^10.x (modular architecture)
- PostgreSQL + TypeORM ^0.3.x
- Socket.IO ^4.x (real-time chat, agent streaming)
- JWT + Passport (auth)
- Zod ^3.x (runtime validation via `nestjs-zod`)
- `@nestjs/config` (env management)
- Bull + Redis (background jobs, voice queue, caching)
- bcrypt (password hashing)
- date-fns (date/time manipulation)

### Frontend (frontend-user & admin)
- Next.js 16+ (App Router) + React 19+ + TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS v4
- next-intl ^4.x (i18n: ru, en, pl, es, de)
- SWR ^2.x (data fetching and caching)
- react-hook-form ^7.x + Zod (forms)
- Radix UI primitives
- Zustand ^5.x (client UI state only — NOT for auth)
- Framer Motion ^12.x (animations)
- socket.io-client ^4.x (real-time)
- Lucide React (icons)
- axios (HTTP client)
- date-fns (date/time formatting and manipulation)

### AI / Voice
- Default LLM: DeepSeek (via OpenAI-compatible API)
- Fallback LLM: OpenAI GPT-4o
- Provider controlled via env: `AI_PROVIDER=deepseek|openai`
- Always abstracted behind `AgentService` — never call provider SDK directly
- STT/TTS: switchable via `VOICE_PROVIDER` env (google|yandex|openai)
- Stream responses (SSE or WebSocket) whenever possible

### Deploy
- Docker containers
- Environment managed via `.env` files; never hardcode secrets
- `docker-compose.yml` structure for local dev (PostgreSQL, Redis, etc.) — define when infrastructure setup begins; AI assistant should ask for guidance at that point

---

## General Coding Principles

### TypeScript
- Always use strict TypeScript (`strict: true` in tsconfig)
- Never use `any` — use `unknown` + type guard, or define an interface
- Export types/interfaces from dedicated `*.types.ts` or `*.interface.ts` files
- Derive TypeScript types from Zod schemas with `z.infer<>`

### Naming Conventions
- Files: `kebab-case` everywhere
- Classes: `PascalCase`
- Variables/functions: `camelCase`
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`
- NestJS modules: one folder per domain (e.g. `src/property/`, `src/booking/`)
- DB entities: suffix `Entity` (e.g. `PropertyEntity`)
- DTOs: suffix `Dto` (e.g. `CreatePropertyDto`)
- Interfaces: prefix `I` only when it conflicts with a class name

### Functions & Components
- Single responsibility: one function/component does one thing
- Max function length ~60 lines — extract helpers if longer
- Prefer `async/await` over raw Promises
- Never swallow errors silently — always log or rethrow with context
- Avoid deep nesting (max 3 levels) — use early returns

---

## Backend Rules (NestJS)

### Module Structure
Every domain module follows this layout:
```
src/<domain>/
├── <domain>.module.ts
├── <domain>.controller.ts
├── <domain>.service.ts
├── <domain>.repository.ts          # optional, for complex queries
├── dto/
│   ├── create-<domain>.dto.ts
│   └── update-<domain>.dto.ts
├── entities/
│   └── <domain>.entity.ts
├── interfaces/
│   └── <domain>.interface.ts
└── constants/
    └── <domain>.constants.ts       # prompts, enums, config keys
```

### Key Domains
- `auth/` — JWT, roles (OWNER, MANAGER, GUEST, SUPERADMIN)
- `user/` — user profiles
- `property/` — rental objects, knowledge base, timezone per property
- `booking/` — reservations, calendar, state machine (see Booking States below)
- `agent/` — AI agent service (text + voice, provider-agnostic)
- `chat/` — message history, WebSocket gateway
- `knowledge-base/` — per-property KB entries, embeddings
- `voice/` — STT/TTS pipeline
- `notification/` — push, email, SMS
- `file/` — file upload, StorageService
- `billing/` — token usage tracking per property

### Config & Environment
- Use `@nestjs/config` with `ConfigModule.forRoot()` registered globally.
- Validate the full env schema with Zod at application startup — fail fast on missing/invalid variables.
- Access env variables ONLY through `ConfigService` — never use `process.env` directly in modules.
- Every new env variable must be documented in `.env.example` with a short comment.
- No secrets, API keys, or JWT material in source code or commits.
- Separate credentials per environment (dev/staging/prod); principle of least privilege for DB users in production.
- Split clearly: `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` (browser-safe) vs server-only variables (LLM keys, DB, JWT).

### Validation & DTOs
- **Use `nestjs-zod` for all DTO validation. Do NOT use `class-validator` or `class-transformer`.**
- Import Zod schemas from `packages/shared` and wrap them with `createZodDto()` to create NestJS-compatible DTOs with automatic Swagger generation.
- Register global `ZodValidationPipe` instead of NestJS default `ValidationPipe`.
- Reject unknown fields via Zod `.strict()` where appropriate.

### API Conventions
- All endpoints under `/api/v1/` prefix
- Swagger enabled; DTOs auto-documented via `nestjs-zod`
- Consistent response shape: `{ data, meta?, error? }`
- Rate limiting via `@nestjs/throttler` on auth and AI endpoints
- Pagination with max page size on all list endpoints; no unbounded queries
- Health check endpoint: `GET /api/v1/health` (for Docker readiness probes)

### Auth & Roles
- Use `@Roles()` decorator + `RolesGuard` on every endpoint
- Roles: `SUPERADMIN | OWNER | MANAGER | GUEST`
- JWT access tokens short-lived (~15 min); refresh token flow for session continuity
- Tokens set as `httpOnly` cookies for frontend consumption. Do not return raw JWTs in JSON body unless specifically for mobile/external API clients.
- Passwords: bcrypt or argon2 only; never plaintext
- Rate-limit auth endpoints (login, password reset) per IP and per identifier

### Multi-tenancy & Access Control
- Every query/mutation on tenant data must be scoped by property (and/or organization). Never trust client-sent IDs without verifying ownership in the service layer.
- Prevent IDOR: never return or mutate resources by ID alone without verifying access.
- `SUPERADMIN` bypasses tenant scope ONLY in explicit admin modules; default is deny.
- Prefer UUIDs in public APIs; never expose sequential internal IDs.

### Database
- TypeORM with migrations — **never** `synchronize: true` in production
- UUID primary keys everywhere
- Soft deletes (`DeleteDateColumn`) only where product requires audit trail; document per entity
- All timestamps: `createdAt`, `updatedAt` via `@CreateDateColumn` / `@UpdateDateColumn`
- Migration naming: `<timestamp>-<kebab-case-description>.ts` (e.g. `1711700000000-add-property-timezone.ts`). Generate via `typeorm migration:generate`.
- Seed data: maintain seed scripts in `src/database/seeds/` for development environments. Each seed file exports an async function; runner in `src/database/seed.ts`. Never run seeds in production automatically.

### Dates & Timezones
- **Store all timestamps in UTC** in the database (`timestamp with time zone` / `timestamptz` column type in PostgreSQL).
- Every `PropertyEntity` has a `timezone` field (IANA format, e.g. `Europe/Warsaw`, `America/New_York`).
- Backend always operates in UTC internally. Convert to property timezone only when producing user-facing output (API responses, notifications).
- Use `date-fns` + `date-fns-tz` for all date manipulation and timezone conversion. Never use `moment` or native `Date` arithmetic.
- Booking check-in/check-out times: store as UTC, but interpret in context of property timezone.
- Frontend receives UTC, converts to display timezone via property's `timezone` field.

### Money & Currency
- **Store all monetary values as integers in minor units** (e.g. cents: $19.99 → `1999`). Never use `float` or `decimal` for money in application code.
- Database column type: `integer` (or `bigint` for large sums).
- Currency stored as ISO 4217 code (e.g. `USD`, `EUR`, `PLN`) alongside amount.
- Format for display only on the frontend using `Intl.NumberFormat`.
- Multi-currency support deferred; initial implementation assumes single currency per property.

### Logging
- Use NestJS built-in `Logger` — never `console.log` in production code.
- **Correlation ID:** generate a unique `requestId` (UUID) per HTTP request via middleware; attach to every log entry, error response, and downstream service call (including AI).
- Log format: structured JSON in production (`{ timestamp, level, requestId, userId?, propertyId?, message, ...context }`).
- Log levels: `error` for failures requiring action, `warn` for recoverable issues, `log` for business events, `debug` for development detail, `verbose` for deep tracing.
- Never log: passwords, JWT tokens, full API keys, credit card data, raw audio, full LLM prompts in production.
- Always log: requestId, userId, propertyId, operation name, duration for slow operations (>500ms).

### Error Handling
- Global `ExceptionFilter` catches all unhandled errors and returns consistent shape: `{ statusCode, message, error, requestId, timestamp, path }`
- Zod validation errors → 400 with field-level details
- TypeORM unique constraint violations → 409 Conflict
- Unauthorized → 401; Forbidden → 403; Not Found → 404
- Never leak internal stack traces or DB details in production responses

### Caching
- Use Redis (same instance as Bull) for application-level caching.
- Cache strategy per use case:
  - **KB embeddings / search results:** cache with TTL, invalidate on KB update
  - **Property metadata:** short TTL (5 min), read-heavy
  - **User session data:** via auth cookies, not Redis cache
- Use NestJS `CacheModule` with Redis store, or a thin wrapper service.
- Cache keys follow pattern: `<domain>:<entityId>:<qualifier>` (e.g. `property:uuid:metadata`, `kb:uuid:embeddings`).
- Always provide cache invalidation alongside cache writes.

### AI / Agent
- All LLM calls go through `AgentService` — NEVER call OpenAI/DeepSeek SDK in controllers or other services.
- Provider selected at runtime from `AI_PROVIDER` env via factory/strategy pattern.
- Always set `max_tokens`, `temperature`, and `timeout` explicitly per use case.
- Prompts live in `constants/<domain>-prompt.ts` as typed template functions, not raw strings.
- Treat user/guest content as untrusted: separate system instructions from user messages (prompt-injection defense).
- Model output shown in UI must be sanitized via safe markdown renderer; never `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.
- **Token billing:** every AI request MUST save token usage to `TokenUsageEntity` tied to `propertyId` and `userId` for tenant billing and quota enforcement. Console logging alone is insufficient.
- Per-user and per-property rate limiting on agent/chat endpoints.
- Do not log full prompts or PII in production; use structured logs (requestId, userId, propertyId, tokenCount).

### Voice
- **Flow 1 (Voice Messages):** client records audio via MediaRecorder API → uploads via HTTP Multipart → backend STT → same agent pipeline as text → optional TTS response.
- **Flow 2 (Real-time Calls):** deferred to later phase. When implemented: audio chunks streamed via WebSocket binary for low latency; never HTTP uploads for real-time voice.
- Validate audio MIME type, duration, and max upload size on the server.

### File Storage
- Abstract behind `StorageService` interface (`FileStoragePort`) with methods: `put`, `getUrl`, `delete`, `exists`.
- Initial implementation: local filesystem (`STORAGE_PROVIDER=local`).
- Interface designed so S3-compatible backend (AWS S3, MinIO) can replace local without changing consumers.
- Server-side validation: MIME type whitelist + max file size per upload type.
- Define retention and deletion policy; store audio only if product requires it.

### Background Jobs (Bull)
- Use `@nestjs/bull` for all asynchronous/heavy processing: STT/TTS, email sending, KB embedding generation, scheduled notifications.
- Queue naming: `<domain>-<action>` in kebab-case (e.g. `voice-transcription`, `kb-embedding`, `notification-email`).
- Each queue has a dedicated processor class: `<Domain><Action>Processor` (e.g. `VoiceTranscriptionProcessor`).
- Retry strategy: max 3 attempts with exponential backoff. Failed jobs after all retries go to failed state for manual inspection.
- Use Bull Board (`@bull-board/nestjs`) in development for queue monitoring (disabled or auth-protected in production).
- Never put long-running tasks (>5s) in the HTTP request cycle — always enqueue.

### Booking States
Bookings follow a strict state machine. Only allowed transitions:
```
PENDING → CONFIRMED → CHECKED_IN → CHECKED_OUT
PENDING → CANCELLED
PENDING → DECLINED
CONFIRMED → CANCELLED
CONFIRMED → NO_SHOW
```
- State stored as enum column on `BookingEntity`.
- Transitions enforced in `BookingService` — controller never sets state directly.
- Each transition emits a domain event (e.g. `BookingConfirmedEvent`) for side effects (notifications, calendar sync).
- `cancelledBy` field tracks who cancelled (guest, manager, system).
- `CHECKED_OUT` and `CANCELLED`/`DECLINED`/`NO_SHOW` are terminal states.

### WebSocket / Real-time (Socket.IO)
- Authenticate WebSocket connections via JWT from `httpOnly` cookie or handshake auth header.
- Namespace per domain: `/chat`, `/notifications` (future: `/voice`).
- Room naming: `property:{propertyId}`, `chat:{chatId}`.
- Event naming: `camelCase` — `message:send`, `message:received`, `agent:streamChunk`, `agent:streamEnd`.
- Always emit error events on failure instead of silently dropping.

### Domain Events
- Use NestJS `EventEmitter2` for internal cross-module communication (e.g. booking confirmed → notification, agent call → token usage persist).
- Event classes in `src/common/events/` with suffix `Event` (e.g. `BookingConfirmedEvent`).

### Feature Flags
- Use environment-based feature flags for gradual rollout of major features (voice, new AI models, experimental UI).
- Feature flags accessed via `ConfigService` with prefix `FF_` (e.g. `FF_VOICE_ENABLED=true`).
- Check flags in service layer, not in controllers or UI components directly.
- When a feature is fully rolled out, remove the flag and dead code in the next release cycle.

---

## Frontend Rules (Next.js App Router)

### File & Folder Structure
```
src/
├── app/
│   ├── [locale]/          # i18n routing (next-intl)
│   └── api/               # route handlers if needed
├── components/
│   ├── ui/                # shared primitives (Button, Input, Modal...)
│   ├── layout/            # AppShell, Sidebar, Header, Footer
│   ├── property/          # property-specific components
│   ├── chat/              # chat UI components
│   ├── agent/             # agent widget components
│   └── forms/             # reusable form components
├── hooks/                 # custom React hooks
├── lib/
│   ├── api/               # API client (axios instance, interceptors)
│   ├── socket/            # socket.io client setup
│   └── utils/             # helpers, formatters, date utils
├── stores/                # Zustand stores (UI state only)
├── types/                 # frontend-only types (prefer packages/shared)
└── messages/              # i18n (ru.json, en.json, pl.json, es.json, de.json)
```

### Component Rules
- Prefer Server Components by default; use `'use client'` only when necessary (events, state, browser API)
- Extract reusable UI to `components/ui/` — never duplicate primitives
- Props interfaces defined inline or in collocated `*.types.ts`
- Never fetch data directly inside components — use SWR hooks or server-side data functions
- Use `next/image` for all images, `next/link` for all internal navigation

### Responsive Design
- **Mobile-first approach.** Write base styles for mobile, then layer on `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:` breakpoints.
- Use Tailwind CSS default breakpoints: `sm` (640px), `md` (768px), `lg` (1024px), `xl` (1280px), `2xl` (1536px).
- Dashboard layout: collapsible sidebar on `lg+`, bottom navigation or hamburger menu on mobile.
- All pages and components must be usable on 375px width (iPhone SE minimum).
- Touch targets: minimum 44x44px for interactive elements on mobile.
- Use responsive Tailwind utilities instead of CSS media queries. Avoid `hidden`/`block` toggling for large component trees — prefer conditional rendering or responsive layout shifts.
- Test on real device widths, not just resized browser.

### UI Patterns: Loading, Error, Empty States
- **Loading:** use skeleton components (`components/ui/skeleton.tsx`) that mirror the shape of the content. Never show a blank page or a lone spinner for full-page loads.
- **Error:** use `ErrorBoundary` wrapper for client components. Display a user-friendly message with a retry action. Show `requestId` for support reference.
- **Empty state:** every list/table/feed must have a designed empty state component with an icon, message, and primary action (e.g. "No properties yet — add your first property").
- Standard components: `<Skeleton />`, `<ErrorState />`, `<EmptyState />` in `components/ui/`.
- API error responses display via toast notifications (e.g. `sonner` or Radix Toast); do not use `alert()`.

### Accessibility
- Use Radix UI primitives for all interactive components — they provide ARIA attributes, keyboard navigation, and focus management out of the box.
- All images must have descriptive `alt` text (or `alt=""` for decorative).
- Color contrast: meet WCAG AA (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text). Verify with Tailwind's color palette.
- Focusable elements must have visible focus indicators (Tailwind `focus-visible:ring-*`).
- Forms: every input must have an associated `<label>`. Use `aria-describedby` for error messages.
- Avoid conveying information by color alone — supplement with icons or text.

### Auth & Route Protection
- JWT tokens stored in `httpOnly` cookies (set by backend).
- Route protection via Next.js `middleware.ts` — read cookie, verify, redirect unauthenticated users.
- Server Components extract auth from cookies to pre-fetch user data.
- **Zustand is NOT the source of truth for auth.** It only holds hydrated client-side UI state.

### State Management
- **Zustand:** client UI state only (theme, sidebar toggle, active property context, chat draft)
- **SWR:** all server data fetching with cache invalidation
- **URL search params:** filter/pagination state (use `nuqs` if needed)

### Data Fetching
- SWR for all client-side data fetching. Do not mix in TanStack Query.
- Define SWR hooks in `hooks/use-<domain>.ts` (e.g. `useProperties`, `useBookings`).
- Axios instance in `lib/api/client.ts` with base URL, interceptors, and error transform.

### Forms
- All forms: `react-hook-form` + Zod resolver
- Zod schemas imported from `packages/shared` or defined in `lib/validation/<domain>.schema.ts`
- Share validation between frontend and backend via `packages/shared`

### i18n
- All user-facing strings go through `next-intl` — no hardcoded text
- Message keys follow `feature.element.state` pattern (e.g. `property.form.namePlaceholder`)
- All 5 locales (ru, en, pl, es, de) must have complete message files
- Default locale: `ru`

### Chat & Agent UI
- Chat messages rendered with `react-markdown` (sanitized)
- Streaming agent responses displayed token-by-token via WebSocket or SSE
- Voice input: browser MediaRecorder → upload → STT → agent response
- Voice output: TTS audio URL or stream → HTML5 Audio
- Never block UI during AI processing — always show loading/streaming state

---

## Admin Panel (Next.js)
- Same tech stack and conventions as `frontend-user`
- May share UI primitives via `packages/shared` or future `packages/ui`
- Restricted to `SUPERADMIN` role
- Features: user management, property overview, system config, token usage analytics, audit logs

---

## packages/shared

- Contains: Zod schemas, inferred TS types, shared constants, utility functions
- Imported via `workspace:*` — raw TypeScript, no build step during development
- Add `tsup` build only when explicit `dist/` or publication is needed
- One Zod major version across entire monorepo (currently Zod ^3.x)
- Structure:
```
packages/shared/
├── src/
│   ├── schemas/       # Zod schemas per domain
│   ├── types/         # Inferred types (z.infer<>)
│   ├── constants/     # Shared enums, config keys, booking states
│   └── utils/         # Pure utility functions
├── package.json
└── tsconfig.json
```

---

## Security

### Transport
- Production: HTTPS only; enforce HSTS where host supports it
- CORS: explicit allowlist of origins; never wildcard `*` with credentials
- Helmet middleware on all NestJS apps
- Global request body size limit (e.g. 1 MB for JSON, separate higher limit for file uploads). Reject oversized payloads early.

### Cookies & CSRF
- Auth cookies MUST have all security attributes: `httpOnly`, `Secure` (HTTPS only), `SameSite=Lax`, scoped `Path=/api`.
- CSRF defense: `SameSite=Lax` as baseline + server-side `Origin`/`Referer` header validation on state-changing requests (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE).
- Refresh tokens: on password change, logout, or suspicious activity — invalidate ALL refresh tokens for that user. Store refresh token hashes in DB or Redis for revocation capability.

### WebSocket Security
- Authenticate connections via JWT from `httpOnly` cookie or handshake auth.
- Rate-limit WebSocket events per connection (e.g. max 30 events/min for chat messages).
- Set `maxHttpBufferSize` in Socket.IO config (e.g. 1 MB) to prevent oversized payloads.
- Validate `Origin` header on WS handshake; reject unknown origins.

### File Upload Security
- Never use user-provided filenames for storage paths — generate UUID-based names on the server to prevent path traversal.
- Store uploads outside the web-servable directory; serve via signed URLs or a dedicated endpoint with access checks.
- Validate file content (magic bytes), not just MIME header or extension.

### AI-specific
- LLM API keys never in browser; all calls server-side
- Separate system instructions from user messages (prompt-injection defense)
- Sanitize model output before rendering; never `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`
- Enforce timeouts, max tokens, per-user and per-property quotas

### Data & Privacy
- Minimize PII collection; define retention and deletion for guest/manager data
- Logs: no passwords, tokens, credit card data, unnecessary PII; use structured logging with correlation IDs

### Security Audit Events
- Log security-relevant events to a dedicated audit trail (DB table or structured log stream):
  - Failed login attempts (with IP, user identifier)
  - Password changes, role changes
  - Property creation/deletion, data exports
  - Superadmin actions (who, what, when, which resource)
- Include `requestId`, `userId`, `ip`, `userAgent` in each audit entry.

### Dependencies
- Keep dependencies patched; run `pnpm audit` in CI
- Swagger/debug endpoints disabled or protected in production

### Incident Readiness
- Document how to rotate LLM, JWT, and third-party keys without redeploying unrelated secrets
- Default to server-side tokens and scoped credentials for new integrations

---

## Testing

### Backend (Jest)
- Unit tests for all services (mock repos and external services)
- E2E tests for controller endpoints (`@nestjs/testing` + Supertest)
- Test files: `*.spec.ts` (unit), `*.e2e-spec.ts` (e2e)
- Coverage target: >= 70% for core modules (auth, agent, property, booking)
- Test edge cases: missing fields, unauthorized access, AI provider errors, tenant isolation

### Frontend (Vitest + React Testing Library)
- Unit tests for hooks, utils, Zustand stores
- Component tests for critical UI (chat, forms)
- Playwright for E2E flows (login, property CRUD, agent chat)

